[Music]  the Internet's median John Oliver issued  a call to action on net neutrality on a  recent edition of last week tonight  he urged people to flood the Federal  Communications Commission with comments  to defend the regulation well today's  some of the biggest tech companies like  Facebook and Google are participating  what they call a day of action it is a  protest of the FCC's plan to rollback  Obama era net neutrality rules now the  rules adopted in 2015 increased  government oversight and required online  service providers to treat all Internet  traffic the same they prohibit the  slowing or blocking of websites content  while treating other sites differently  but in May the republican-led FCC voted  to start changing net neutrality rules  CBS News contributor Nicholas Thompson  is editor in chief of Wired and joins us  at the table  hey Nick good morning Connie first give  us a net neutrality 101 lesson very  quickly and why it matters so net  neutrality is the principle that all the  information that flow over the pipes  that come on to your computer right so  when you type in a website information  flows there are pipes it comes to a  computer net neutrality is the principle  that all that information has to be  treated the same so the companies that  own the pipes can't say you know what we  don't want that website to be fast we  want that one to be slow and we don't  like what that one is saying so we're  gonna stop it all together and maybe  you'd like a tiered plan so you can get  Wikipedia right so net neutrality is a  very important principle to people on  the Internet  so who are the companies that are  pushing a repeal of net neutrality no  the people who own the pipes right  Verizon Comcast Time Warner Cable AT&amp;T  though their position is kind of  complicated right now but basically if  you own pipes you want to be able to  make a lot of money off of them by  charging websites to travel it up and  they're pointers that we own and we can  do we should be able to do what we want  with them and the government should be  involved and if we make more money we'll  invest more heavily in them and you'll  get faster speeds we have had the  principle of net neutrality though  almost since the beginning of the  internet and it's flourished pretty  oh it's working out pretty well it's a  very innovative open space so we reached  out to the FCC and they said they did  not have a comment about the day of  action what's their argument well what  the FCC is saying is look number one  let's not regulate right in order to  protect the pipes and to make sure that  they're free you have to pass regulation  so if you're totally against regulation  you might say that number two they're  very close to the telecom companies they  want to do what's right for the telecom  companies they will argue that if you  give the telecom companies more freedom  to control their pipes  they'll innovate more what everybody  else is saying is no every time you  relax and let the telecom companies do  what they want they stifle innovation  it's not good for everyone and in fact  the sort of the core point of net  neutrality is that the Internet has been  a place of openness it's a place where  you can start a company where you can do  what you want and you can be reasonably  certain that if you put a website on the  Internet people will be able to get to  it but if we don't have net neutrality  and you start a company that is opposed  to something that Comcast is running and  it's a competitor to something that Time  Warner Cable has they can shut you down  so it's not just competition it's the  entire principle of the internet that's  why people are so riled up about that  have Facebook and Google have a  complicated relationship with net  neutrality they do because all of their  engineers are in favor of it and the  corporate executives maybe they like  rules that favor big companies so  they're in a complicated spot that they  are behind this today all right Nick  Thompson thank you very much thank you

